
Phase 1 — Fundamentals (1–2 days)
Start with the mental model. Understand what a database is, the difference between a database and a DBMS, and the major families: relational (SQL) vs non-relational (NoSQL). Know when you'd reach for each. Learn the basic vocabulary: tables, rows, columns, schema, primary key, foreign key.
Phase 2 — SQL (the heaviest interview area, 1–2 weeks)
This is where most interviews live, so go deep. Work through:

Basic queries: SELECT, WHERE, ORDER BY, LIMIT, DISTINCT
Joins: INNER, LEFT, RIGHT, FULL, SELF, CROSS — be able to draw what each returns
Aggregations: GROUP BY, HAVING, COUNT/SUM/AVG/MIN/MAX
Subqueries (correlated vs non-correlated) and CTEs (WITH clause)
Window functions: ROW_NUMBER, RANK, DENSE_RANK, LAG, LEAD, partitioning — these come up a lot in mid/senior interviews
Set operations: UNION, UNION ALL, INTERSECT, EXCEPT
DDL/DML/DCL/TCL categories and what commands fall under each

Practice writing queries by hand without an IDE, since whiteboard/screen-share rounds expect that.
Phase 3 — Database Design (3–4 days)

ER diagrams and relationships (one-to-one, one-to-many, many-to-many)
Normalization: 1NF, 2NF, 3NF, BCNF — know the purpose and be able to normalize a messy table
Denormalization and why you'd intentionally do it
Keys: primary, foreign, candidate, composite, surrogate vs natural

Phase 4 — Indexing (2–3 days)

What an index is and the tradeoff (faster reads, slower writes, more storage)
Clustered vs non-clustered indexes
B-tree vs hash indexes, composite indexes
When indexes don't help, and how to read a query execution plan (EXPLAIN)

Phase 5 — Transactions & Concurrency (3–4 days)

ACID properties (be ready to explain each with an example)
Isolation levels: Read Uncommitted, Read Committed, Repeatable Read, Serializable
Concurrency problems: dirty reads, non-repeatable reads, phantom reads
Locking (shared vs exclusive), deadlocks, optimistic vs pessimistic concurrency

Phase 6 — NoSQL (2–3 days)

The four types: key-value (Redis), document (MongoDB), column-family (Cassandra), graph (Neo4j)
When to choose NoSQL over SQL
BASE vs ACID

Phase 7 — Scaling & Distributed Databases (3–4 days, more for senior roles)

Replication (master-slave, master-master), sharding, partitioning
CAP theorem and the PACELC extension
Caching strategies, read replicas
Eventual vs strong consistency

Phase 8 — Practice & Mock Interviews (ongoing)

Grind SQL problems on LeetCode (Database section), HackerRank, or StrataScratch
Review common conceptual Q&A ("difference between DELETE/TRUNCATE/DROP," "what is a deadlock," etc.)
Do timed mock queries
